    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Searching for <strong>under desk elliptical before and after</strong> results usually turns up dramatic transformation claims. The real picture is quieter but still worth it. Used daily, a seated elliptical changes how your body feels during the workday within a week or two, builds a lasting habit within a month, and can contribute to modest weight change over a few months.</p>
<p>What it will not do is reshape your body on its own. This timeline walks through what realistically changes in week one, month one and month three of daily use, the math behind the weight question, and the things you should not expect. It assumes about 60 to 120 minutes of pedaling on workdays, split into short blocks.</p>

<h2>Before You Start: Set a Realistic Baseline</h2>
<p>To see your own before and after, record a few simple things on day one. Skip the dramatic photos and focus on numbers you can compare fairly later.</p>
<ul>
<li>Your weight, measured at the same time of day, plus a waist measurement if you want one.</li>
<li>How stiff your legs and lower back feel at 4 p.m. on a 1 to 10 scale.</li>
<li>Your energy level after lunch on the same scale.</li>
<li>How many minutes you can pedal at moderate resistance before you want to stop.</li>
</ul>
<p>Write them down and check again at weeks 1, 4 and 12. Comparing these honest numbers is far more motivating than chasing a transformation photo.</p>

<h2>Week 1: How Your Body Feels Changes First</h2>
<h3>Energy and the afternoon slump</h3>
<p>Many owners report that the first noticeable change is how the afternoon feels. Gentle pedaling during the post-lunch dip keeps you from going completely still. This varies from person to person, so treat it as a possible bonus rather than a guarantee.</p>
<h3>Stiffness</h3>
<p>Legs that normally feel heavy or stiff by late afternoon often feel looser after a few days of regular pedaling. Your knees, hips and ankles are simply moving more often.</p>
<h3>Mild muscle fatigue</h3>
<p>The front of your thighs may feel tired, and if you pedal backward, the front of your shins too. That is normal and usually fades within a week or two. Sharp or joint pain is not normal: lower the resistance, check your knee clearance and stop if it continues. Our guide to <a href="/what-muscles-does-an-under-desk-elliptical-work/">what muscles an under desk elliptical works</a> explains which muscles you are feeling.</p>
<h3>Setup friction</h3>
<p>Week one is when most people quit, usually because the machine slides, the chair rolls or knees bump the desk. Fix those early with a [[amazon:cubii-mat|non-slip mat]] and [[amazon:caster-cups|caster cups]] for rolling chairs.</p>

<h2>Weeks 2 and 3: The Motivation Dip</h2>
<p>Novelty wears off fast. Somewhere in the second or third week, many people find themselves pedaling less, usually on busy days when they forget the machine is there. This is the most important stretch of the whole timeline, because the habit is not yet automatic.</p>
<p>Two things help. First, tie pedaling to specific tasks rather than to willpower: every call, every inbox session, every video. Second, lower the bar on bad days. Ten minutes at light resistance keeps the streak alive and is far better than skipping. If you track your minutes, look at the weekly total rather than any single day.</p>
<p>This is also when small annoyances decide whether you keep going. A squeak, a machine that creeps forward or knees brushing the desk will push you to stop. Fix them now instead of putting up with them. Our guide on <a href="/quietest-under-desk-elliptical/">the quietest under desk ellipticals</a> helps if noise is the problem.</p>

<h2>Month 1: The Habit Forms</h2>
<h3>Pedaling becomes automatic</h3>
<p>By the end of the first month, pedaling during calls or email starts to feel normal rather than something you have to remember. That shift matters more than any single number, because every later result depends on it.</p>
<p>Tying sessions to a timer helps. Set pedal and rest blocks below and let the chime handle the reminders:</p>
[[tool:timer]]
<h3>Endurance improves</h3>
<p>The resistance that felt like work in week one starts to feel easy. Most people can pedal longer at the same setting, or move up a level or two. That is a sign of better muscular endurance in the legs, mostly the quadriceps.</p>
<h3>The first weight question</h3>
<p>At a moderate pace, two hours a workday adds roughly 1,000 to 1,500 extra calories a week for many adults, compared with sitting. Using the rough rule that about 3,500 calories equals a pound of fat, that is roughly 1 to 2 pounds by the end of month one, if your eating does not change. Early scale changes also include water, so do not read too much into a single weigh-in.</p>

<h2>Month 3: Under Desk Elliptical Results Add Up</h2>
<h3>Weight math over 12 weeks</h3>
<p>Over three months, the same daily routine may add up to roughly 3 to 6 pounds of fat loss for many people, again assuming eating stays steady. The 3,500-calorie rule tends to overestimate over longer periods, because your body uses a bit less energy as you get lighter and appetite can creep up with activity. Expect the low end and treat anything more as a bonus.</p>
<p>Run your own numbers with the <a href="/tools/under-desk-elliptical-calorie-calculator/">under desk elliptical calorie calculator</a>, and see the full breakdown in <a href="/can-you-lose-weight-with-under-desk-elliptical/">can you lose weight with an under desk elliptical</a>.</p>
<h3>Sitting feels different</h3>
<p>After three months, many owners say that sitting still for long periods feels uncomfortable, in a good way. They notice when they have not moved and reach for the pedals. That is the real "after": a workday with far less dead sitting time.</p>
<h3>Fitness carry-over</h3>
<p>Stairs and longer walks may feel a little easier thanks to better leg endurance. The effect is modest, and it is strongest if you were fairly inactive before you started.</p>
<p>Month three is also a good time to raise the bar slightly. If your sessions feel effortless, add a level of resistance during calls, try short faster intervals, or add a daily walk. Your body adapts to the routine, so small increases keep the results coming instead of plateauing.</p>

<h2>Under Desk Elliptical Before and After: A Summary Table</h2>
